Here is the **cleanest, simplest C++ code** that explains *everything* you learned — **class declaration, object creation, accessing members, data hiding** — with very clear comments.

---

# ⭐ **Simple C++ Program (With Full Comments)**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// ************ CLASS DECLARATION ************
class Item
{
    // ---- PRIVATE SECTION (data hiding) ----
    // Outside code cannot access these directly
    int number;
    float cost;

public:
    // ---- PUBLIC FUNCTIONS ----
    // Used to access and modify private data

    // Function to input values
    void getData(int a, float b)
    {
        number = a;   // allowed (inside class)
        cost = b;     // allowed
    }

    // Function to display values
    void putData()
    {
        cout << "Number = " << number << endl;
        cout << "Cost = " << cost << endl;
    }
};

// ************ MAIN FUNCTION ************
int main()
{
    // ---- OBJECT DECLARATION ----
    Item x;     // x is an object of class Item

    // ---- ACCESSING MEMBER FUNCTIONS ----
    x.getData(100, 75.5);   // calling public function to set private data
    x.putData();            // calling public function to display data

    return 0;
}
```

---

# ⭐ **What This Code Shows (Simple Explanation)**

### ✔ 1. Class Declaration  
The class `Item` contains:
- two **private** data members (`number`, `cost`)
- two **public** functions (`getData`, `putData`)

### ✔ 2. Data Hiding  
You **cannot** do:
```cpp
x.number = 10;   // ERROR
```
because `number` is private.

### ✔ 3. Object Creation  
```cpp
Item x;
```
creates an object `x` with its own `number` and `cost`.

### ✔ 4. Accessing Class Members  
You use public functions:

```cpp
x.getData(100, 75.5);
x.putData();
```

This is how objects "communicate" in OOP.

---

# ⭐ Perfect One-Line Exam Summary  
**Private data is accessed only through public member functions using the dot operator and an object.**

